<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhao Tuo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 137 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhao Tuo was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> general sent to conquer the far south who founded the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, with its capital at Panyu (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>), in 204 BCE. Ruling Chinese settlers and Yue peoples, he adopted local customs, accepted Han overlordship in 196 BCE and later styled himself emperor. He is said to have reigned for some seventy years before his death in 137 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo.jpg" alt="Zhao Tuo" width="320" height="517" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Chen (Iamdavidtheking 11:50, 10 September 2007 (UTC)) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_statue_of_Zhao_Tuo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhao Tuo was born around 240 BCE in Zhengding, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, in a region annexed by the Huaxia state of Zhao. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered Zhao in 222 BCE he entered Qin service and became a general in its conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> peoples. The conquered Yue lands were divided into the provinces of Guilin, Nanhai and Xiang, and Zhao served as magistrate in Nanhai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nanhai's military commander, Ren Xiao, fell ill and advised Zhao to stay out of the affairs of the declining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and to build an independent kingdom around the remote city of Panyu, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. Zhao closed the roads at Hengpu, Yangshan and Huangqi, replaced the Qin officials with his own appointees and, by the time Qin fell in 206 BCE, had conquered Guilin and Xiang as well. He declared himself king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, the Southern Yue.</p><p>In 196 BCE Emperor Gaozu of Han sent Lu Jia to recognise Zhao as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> in return for nominal submission. In 185 BCE Empress Lü's officials banned the trade of iron and horses with Nanyue, and Zhao proclaimed himself emperor and attacked the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>. A Han army sent in 181 BCE failed to cross the mountains because heat and damp sickened its men. Zhao then menaced Minyue, Western Ou and Luo until they submitted. In late 180 BCE Emperor Wen of Han tried to appease him, tending the graves of Zhao's parents at Zhengding and appointing his cousins to high office. Zhao dropped the imperial title in dealing with the Han but kept it at home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The conquest of Âu Lạc is told differently in different sources. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> mention neither King An Dương nor a military conquest, while later records and a legend tell of a turtle-claw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a> that gave its holder power. In the legend Zhao's son Zhong Shi married An Dương's daughter, tampered with the crossbow's trigger and returned to his father, who then defeated An Dương. Zhao left the local chiefs in place and posted two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a>, in the Red River Delta and the Mã and Cả river area. For the first time the region formed part of a polity headed by a Chinese ruler.</p><p>Zhao died in 137 BCE, reportedly aged 103, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-mo">Zhao Mo</a> succeeded him. Some traditional Vietnamese historians counted him founder of the Triệu dynasty, while some contemporary historians contest that he was a foreign invader.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhao Tuo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 179 BCE <b>Zhao Tuo</b>, ruler of Nanyue, conquered Au Lac; legend says his son married An Duong's daughter My Chau and stole the secret of the magic crossbow.</blockquote><a href="/state/au-lac">Au Lac</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Zhao Tuo</b> was a Qin officer sent south in the conquest of the Baiyue lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanyue">Nanyue</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhengding County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.15,114.57&amp;year=-147" aria-label="Zhengding County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7403 793 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhao Tuo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7853 1063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7853 1063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.15,114.57&amp;year=-147">What was here in 147 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
